    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Moab fault, a basin-scale normal fault with up to 1 km of stratigraphic offset, is segmented along strike, with segments separated by branch points and steps. Fault branch points are associated with extensive carbonate cementation of the adjacent sandstone whereas one step is associated with predominant silica cementation. Carbonate cementation is observed as concretions and as veins with associated rinds of pore filling carbonate cement. Abundance of concretions and veins and the diameter of concretions decrease with distance from the fault. Carbonate is consistently associated with bleaching of the reddish hematite-cemented sandstones and with remnant hydrocarbons. It is envisioned that fault-related cementation is controlled by two processes: (1) rapid upward fluid flow along the fault and (2) degradation and microbial oxidation of hydrocarbons in contact with meteoric water. Evidence for rapid fluid flow is provided by clastic dikes associated with the fault. Cooling of rapidly upward flowing fluid would favor silica precipitation whereas decompression and CO2 exsolution would lead to carbonate precipitation. Evidence for carbonate precipitation due to hydrocarbon degradation is observed through carbonate precipitated along contacts of residual oil stains. Microbially mediated carbonate precipitation results in formation of carbonate concretions and discontinuous fracture cement. It is inferred that the discontinuous occurrence of microbially mediated carbonate cement at the contact of oil with meteoric water may impede hydrocarbon migration along fault conduits but is inefficient in completely sealing migration pathways. Carbonate cement by rapid CO2 exsolution along fault surfaces, on the other hand, may be more efficient in sealing fault conduits.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
